<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Havana Hopewell culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">200 BCE–400 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Havana Hopewell</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> Hopewell culture was a Hopewellian people who lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> River and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> valleys in Iowa, Illinois and Missouri from 200 BCE to 400 CE. It was one complex within the Hopewell exchange system, which began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> and Illinois River valleys around 300 BCE and carried obsidian from the Yellowstone area, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from Lake Superior and shells from the Gulf Coast. Its sites include the Toolesboro Mound Group in Louisa County, Iowa, whose conical burial mounds were built between 100 BCE and 200 CE beside a large octagonal earthen enclosure, and the Ogden-Fettie Site in Illinois, with thirty-five mounds in a crescent-shaped enclosure.</p></div>
